#########################################################
DOCUMENT();
## Initializations: (Required)
loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "PGchoicemacros.pl",
  "parserRadioButtons.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "parserMultiAnswer.pl",
  "RserveClient.pl",
  "answerHints.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl"
);

#########################################################
## Setup: this is where we use Perl and PG objects (Required)


###Random number generator seed based on student's login. No need to change.
Context("Numeric");
# warn("Student name: $studentName, login: $studentLogin, id: $studentID");
my $hash = crypt($studentLogin, 'a1');
# warn("crypt: $hash");
@nums = (0..9,'a'..'z','A'..'Z');
my %nums = map { $nums[$_] => $_ } 0..$#nums;
my $seed = 0;
$seed = $seed * 62 + $nums{$_} foreach split(//, substr($hash, -5, 5));
##warn("seed: " . $seed);

####Call R server
rserve_eval("set.seed($seed)"); ## can not combine with the rest

@a=rserve_eval('
a <- round(runif(1, min=0.01, max=0.1),2)
');

@am=rserve_eval('
1-a
');


@b=rserve_eval('
b <- round(runif(1, min=0.2, max=0.4),2)
');

@bm=rserve_eval('
1-b
');

@q1=rserve_eval('
re1<-(a^2  + (1-a)^2)/(b^2+(1-b)^2)
round(re1, 2)
');

@q2=rserve_eval('
re2<- 0.5/(b^2+(1-b)^2)
round(re2, 2)
');

$plot=rserve_start_plot('png');
rserve_eval('
x<-seq(0, 1, 0.02)
y<-x^2+(1-x)^2
plot(x, y, type="l", xlab="X", ylab="Y",ylim=c(0, 1), main="Plot of x^2 +(1-x)^2 for x on the unit interval ")
axis(side = 1, at = seq(0.1, 0.9, 0.2))
axis(side = 2, at = seq(0.1, 0.9, 0.2))
');
$plotpath=rserve_finish_plot($plot);




$q1ans =@q1[0];
$q2ans =@q2[0];

#########################################################
## Main Text: where all text goes (Required)
Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
Let \(X\) and \(Y\) be observations taken at random from a distribution with mean \( \mu \)  and variance \( \sigma^2 \). Assume that \(X\) and \(Y\) are independently distributed. Consider the following two point estimators of the population mean \( \mu \):
$BR
$BR
$BBOLD (1) $EBOLD 
$BCENTER
\(
@a[0] X +@am[0] Y    
\)
$ECENTER
$BR
$BBOLD (2) $EBOLD 
$BCENTER
\(
@b[0] X + @bm[0] Y    
\)
$ECENTER
$BR
$BR
You should be able to confirm that both estimators are unbiased. Answer the following, rounding your answers to two decimal places where appropriate.
$BR
$BR
$BBOLD(a) $EBOLD Find the relative efficiency, defined here using the larger variance
in the numerator. \{  ans_rule(7) \} 
$BR
$BR
$BBOLD(b) $EBOLD Find an unbiased estimator of the population mean of the form
\(cX+(1-c)Y \) for some \(0 < c < 1\) that is the most efficient. In the answer box give the relative efficiency of this estimator compared to the more efficient estimator from (1) and (2).  \{  ans_rule(7) \} 
END_TEXT
#########################################################
## Answers evaluation (Required)
ANS( num_cmp($q1ans, tol=> 0.02) );
ANS( num_cmp($q2ans, tol=> 0.02) );

#########################################################
## Solution (Optional but recommended)
Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_SOLUTION
We define the relative efficiency of two estimators, say \( \hat{\theta}_1 \) and \( \hat{\theta}_2 \), as 
$BR
$BCENTER
\(
\frac{\text{Var} (\hat{\theta}_1)  }{ \text{Var} (\hat{\theta}_2)  }
\)
$ECENTER
$BR
when \( \text{Var} (\hat{\theta}_1) > \text{Var} (\hat{\theta}_2)  \), and the reciprocal of the ratio otherwise. 
$BR
$BR
$BBOLD (a) $EBOLD For estimator (1),
$BR
$BR
$BCENTER
\begin{align} 
Var ( @a[0] X + @am[0] Y  ) &= @a[0]^2 Var(X) +@am[0]^2 Var(Y) \\
&= @a[0]^2 \sigma^2 + @am[0]^2 \sigma^2 \\
&= \sigma^2 (@a[0]^2 + @am[0]^2 ). 
\end{align}
$ECENTER
$BR
$BR
Similarly for estimator (2),
$BR
$BR
$BCENTER
\begin{align} 
Var ( @b[0] X +@bm[0] Y  ) &= @b[0]^2 Var(X) + @bm[0]^2 Var(Y) \\
&= @b[0]^2 \sigma^2 + @bm[0]^2 \sigma^2 \\
&= \sigma^2 (@b[0]^2 +  @bm[0]^2 ). 
\end{align}
$ECENTER
$BR
$BR
We see that \( \text{Var} (\hat{\theta}_1) > \text{Var} (\hat{\theta}_2)  \), hence the relative efficiency of the two estimators is     
$BR
$BCENTER
\(
\frac{ @a[0]^2 +  @am[0]^2  }{ @b[0]^2 + @bm[0]^2 } = $q1ans. 
\)
$ECENTER
$BR

$BR
$BBOLD (b) $EBOLD In general, assuming an estimator of the form \( cX + (1-c)Y\) for some constant \(c\), the variance will be \(  \sigma^2 (c^2 +(1-c)^2) \), which as the plot below shows reaches a minimum when \(c = 1/2\). 
 $BR
$BCENTER
\{
image( $plotpath, width=>350, height=>200 )
\}
$ECENTER
$BR
Hence the most efficient estimator will be
$BR
$BCENTER
\(
0.5X + 0.5Y, 
\)
$ECENTER
$BR
which has variance
$BR
$BCENTER
\(
\sigma^2(0.5^2 + 0.5^2)=0.5\sigma^2.  
\)
$ECENTER
$BR
The required relative efficiency is therefore
$BR
$BCENTER
\(
\frac{0.5}{@b[0]^2 + @bm[0]^2} = $q2ans. 
\)
$ECENTER

END_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();